			"text": "in the theory allows room for significant variability in how properties such as thermal conductivity of air, specific heat, etc., are evaluated. As a result, when an experimental spread rate does not agree reasonably well with the theoretical prediction, it is not clear whether the disagreement is due to incorrect use of properties in the formula or due to more fundamental reason such as the flame spread is not in the thermal regime. The opposed-flow flame spread experiments, where the opposing flow velocity can be directly controlled in a flow tunnel (Refs. 11 and 12) can easily create a high flow velocity where gas-phase chemistry becomes important due to shortened residence time. However, the opposed flow cannot be arbitrarily reduced."
