			"pdfPage": 59,
			"reportPage": 51,
			"text": "of PMMA. These results agree well with similar comparisons of 1g and microgravity results for opposed-flow flame spread on wire insulation (Refs. 4 and 5), as well as theoretical considerations of the problem (Ref. 6). The rods were ignited at the downstream end, and the flame was allowed to spread across the rod for a period of time at each velocity. A typical test is shown in Figure 4.4(a) to (e), where the flame is initially sooty. As the flow velocity is reduced, the sooting decreases and then disappears. The flame tail opens up as it continues to spread along the rod. The rod extends past the end of the flame as the spreading continues. This wide-open flame tail with rod remaining behind is unique to microgravity."
